SQL
Lesson 4
Notes
----------

A Database Index is a seperate, specialized data structure (usually a B-Tree/Balanced Tree) that keeps a sorted pointer map to your rows.

Instead of doing a Full Table Scan (Sequential Scan)(O(N) time complexity) which is computationally expensive, an indexed lookup takes a few quick operations to jump straight to the exact row on disk.

Syntax:

CREATE INDEX idx_users_username ON users (username);

Whenever you define a column as a PRIMARY KEY or UNIQUE, the database engine automatically creates an index for that column under the hood.

CREATE TABLE users (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,        --Auto-Indexed
  email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE   --Auto-Indexed
);

Composition Indexes (Multi-Column): Indexes multiple columns in a specific order.

SELECT * FROM reviews
WHERE game_id = 42 AND is_published = true;

CREATE INDEX idx_reviews_game_published
ON reviews (game_id, is_published);

The Left-to-Right Rule: A composite index on (A, B) speeds up queries filtering on (A, B) or just (A), but it WILL NOT help queries that filter on just (B) alone.

Use EXPLAIN ANALYZE to spot slow queries.
PostgreSQL, MySQL, and SQLite provide a diagnostic tool called EXPLAIN (or EXPLAIN ANALYZE in Postgres) that reveals the exact execution plan the database engine uses under the hood.

EXPLAIN ANALYZE
SELECT * FROM game_downloads
WHERE downloaded_at >= '2026-01-01';

What to look for in the output:
- Seq Scan
  - The database scanned every row one by one. Bad for large tables.
- Index Scan / Bitmap Index Scan
  - The database used an index to jump straight to the matching rows. Excellent!
- Execution Time: 450.23ms vs 0.12ms
  - Adding the right index often turns a half-second query into a fraction of a millisecond.


The Golden Trade-off: Don't Index Everything.
If indexes make SELECT queries so fast, why not index every single column in every table?
Because Indexes come at a cost:
- Storage Overhead
  - Every index is a seperate data structure saved to disk/RAM.
- Slower INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE: Every time you write new data, the database must write the row to the table AND rebuild/re-balance every index attached to the table.

Basically:

SELECT(Read) W/O Index = Slow
SELECT(Read) W/ Index = Super Fast
INSERT/UPDATE(Write) W/O Index = Fast
INSERT/UPDATE(Write) W/ Index = Slower(Index rebuild overhead)

Rule of Thumb: Index columns that appear frequently in WHERE, JOIN ... ON, ORDER BY, or GROUP BY clauses - especially on tables with high read volume compared to write volume.



